L000172	\title{Mixed-state topological order and the errorfield double formulation of decoherence-induced transitions}
L000173	
L000174	
L000175	
L000176	
L000177	
L000178	
L000179	
L000180	
L000181	
L000182	\author{Yimu Bao}
L000183	\affiliation{Department of Physics, University of California, Berkeley, CA 94720, USA}
L000184	\author{Ruihua Fan}
L000185	\affiliation{Department of Physics, Harvard University, Cambridge, MA 02138, USA}
L000186	\author{Ashvin Vishwanath}
L000187	\affiliation{Department of Physics, Harvard University, Cambridge, MA 02138, USA}
L000188	\author{Ehud Altman}
L000189	\affiliation{Department of Physics, University of California, Berkeley, CA 94720, USA}
L000190	\affiliation{Materials Sciences Division, Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory, Berkeley, CA 94720, USA}
L000191	
L000192	
L000193	\begin{abstract}
L000194	We develop an effective field theory characterizing the impact of decoherence on states with Abelian topological order and on their capacity to protect quantum information. The decoherence appears as a temporal defect in the double topological quantum field theory that describes the pure density matrix of the uncorrupted state, and it drives a boundary phase transition involving anyon condensation at a critical coupling strength. The ensuing decoherence-induced phases and the loss of quantum information are classified by the Lagrangian subgroups of the double topological order. Our framework generalizes the error recovery transitions, previously derived for certain stabilizer codes, to generic topologically ordered states and shows that they stem from phase transitions in the intrinsic topological order characterizing the mixed state.
L000195	\end{abstract}
L000196	
L000197	\maketitle
L000198	
L000199	Recent experiments in many-body quantum simulation platforms, such as arrays of Rydberg atoms and superconducting qubit systems, have shown compelling evidence for the establishment of topological order~\cite{Semeghini:2021wls,Bluvstein:2021jsq,Satzinger:2021eqy,GoogleQuantumAI:2022fyn}.
L000200	
L000201	
L000202	While these platforms offer a high degree of control, the state preparation is subject to inevitable decoherence, leading ultimately to a mixed state.
L000203	
L000204	Experimental probes of topological order have a long history in the context of the fractional quantum Hall effect in semiconductors~\cite{Tsui:1982yy,AnyonCollisions,AnyonBraid}.
L000205	
L000206	In this case, it is understood that the imperfection due to non-vanishing temperature broadens the signatures of topological order~\cite{xiaogangReview,Nussinov2008PRB,Nussinov2009PNAS,Bravyi2009NoGo,Poulin2013PRL,Brown2016RMP}.
L000207	
L000208	However, the states prepared in these new platforms do not have time to reach thermal equilibrium before they are probed and therefore cannot be described as Gibbs states.
L000209	
L000210	Instead, they are better modeled as a topologically ordered pure state corrupted by local channels describing decoherence for a finite time.
L000211	
L000212	It is natural to ask how to characterize the topological order in such corrupted topological states.
L000213	
L000214	Different perspectives on this problem suggest seemingly conflicting answers.
L000215	
L000216	On one hand, the operation of the local quantum channels has an equivalent description as a finite-depth unitary process in an extended Hilbert space that includes an ancilla qubit for every system qubit.
L000217	
L000218	This finite-depth unitary circuit cannot lead to a singular change in the expectation value of any conventional diagnostic used to probe topological order, such as Wilson loops or open string operators~\cite{Fradkin:2013sab}, which support in a finite region.
L000219	
L000220	From this perspective, the topological order persists for any strength of decoherence, short of full dephasing or depolarization.
L000221	
L000222	
L000223	On the other hand, we can assess the effect of decoherence on the topological order through its ability to destroy the protection of quantum information encoded in the degenerate ground-state subspace~\cite{Kitaev:1997wr,Bravyi:1998sy}.
L000224	
L000225	In this vein, Dennis et al.~\cite{Dennis:2001nw} calculate a finite error threshold for the Toric code subject to local Pauli $X$ and $Z$ errors.
L000226	
L000227	These errors create pairs of syndromes (i.e. anyon excitations) detected by stabilizer measurements.
L000228	
L000229	The recovery scheme is an algorithm for annihilating the syndromes in pairs, which fails when the error rate exceeds the threshold.
L000230	
L000231	The existence of a recovery threshold strongly suggests that there is an underlying decoherence-induced transition in the intrinsic properties of the mixed state, which is not captured by standard probes used to detect ground-state topological order.
L000232	
L000233	We note, however, that the recovery fidelity itself is not an intrinsic property, as it may depend on the recovery algorithm.
L000234	
L000235	Furthermore, theories of recovery thresholds are limited to solvable topological stabilizer codes and do not offer insight into the effect of decoherence on topologically ordered states more generally.
L000236	
L000237	In this Letter, we develop a universal description of topological order and decoherence-induced transitions in corrupted mixed states that is based on the underlying topological quantum field theory (TQFT).
L000238	
L000239	A key step is treating the density matrix as a state vector in a double (ket and bra) Hilbert space, $\kket{\rho}=\mathcal{N}\,|\Psi_0\rangle\otimes|\Psi_0^*\rangle$.
L000240	
L000241	The action of the decoherence channel $\mathcal{N}$, which couples the ket and bra states, can induce anyon condensation in the double space.
L000242	
L000243	We argue that this transition is described in terms of boundary criticality in close analogy to the effect of measurement on quantum ground states analyzed in Ref.~\cite{Garratt:2022ycp}.
L000244	
L000245	To derive an effective theory of the decoherence-induced transition, we utilize the description of the state $|\Psi_0\rangle$ as a (2+1)-dimensional TQFT.
L000246	
L000247	Then, $\kket{\rho}$ is given as a double TQFT coupled by the quantum channel $\mathcal{N}$ only at the temporal boundary $\tau=0$, and the induced transition  corresponds to anyon condensation on the boundary.
L000248	
L000249	Accordingly, the distinct decoherence-induced phases are classified by the possible boundary anyon condensates~\cite{Levin:2013gaa,Barkeshli:2013,Wang:2012am}.
L000250	
L000251	We note that the description in terms of boundary phase transitions explains why the error threshold transitions in stabilizer models invariably map to transitions in 2D classical statistical mechanics or (1+1)D quantum models, despite representing transitions in 2D quantum states~\cite{Dennis:2001nw,Wang:2002ph,Katzgraber:2009zz,Bombin:2012jk,Kubica:2018rab,Chubb:2021htd,CastelnovoPRB2008,Venn:2022kxy,Behrends:2022feh}.
L000252	
L000253	\begin{figure}[t]
L000254	\centering
L000255	\includegraphics[width=0.48\textwidth]{fig1_v2_full.pdf}
L000256	\caption{(a) Errorfield double state $\kket{\rho}$ describing a topologically ordered pure state corrupted by local errors (red dots). (b) Path integral representation of the norm $\bbrakket{\rho}{\rho}$. Two path integrals in the past ($\tau < 0$) and the future ($\tau > 0$) prepare the double ground states at $\tau = 0^-$ and $0^+$. The states are coupled by the quantum channel $\calN^{\dagger}\calN$ (red cuboid). Red lines represent the worldlines of an anyon $\alpha\bar{\alpha}$ created by the incoherent errors. (c) The $\pi/2$ rotation converts the 2D temporal defect at $\tau = 0$ to a (1+1)D spatial defect at $x = 0$. (d) The rotated path integral represents the norm of the ground state of double 2D topological order with a 1D defect.}
L000257	\label{fig:efd}
L000258	\end{figure}
L000259	
L000260	\emph{Errorfield double state.}---
L000261	The basic subject for our analysis is a topologically ordered state $\ket{\Psi_0}$ affected by local decoherence channels $\mathcal{N}_i$:
L000262	\begin{equation}
L000263	    \rho = \prod_i \calN_{i} [\ket{\Psi_0}\bra{\Psi_0}]\,,
L000264	\end{equation}
L000265	where $i$ is a site index.
L000266	
L000267	The local channel $\calN_i[\cdot] := \sum_k \calK_{k,i} (\cdot) \calK_{k,i}^\dagger$ is defined by the Kraus operators $\calK_{k,i}$, which satisfy $\sum_k \calK_{k,i}^\dagger \calK_{k,i} = \mathbb{1}$; they create different kinds of errors in the state.
L000268	
L000269	For example, the channel that causes bit-flip errors consists of two Kraus operators $\calK_{1,i} = \sqrt{1-p}\,\mathbb{1}$ and $\calK_{2,i} = \sqrt{p}X_i$, where $p$ is the error rate.
L000270	
L000271	To understand the effect of decoherence on a quantum ground state, it is convenient to express the Kraus operators in terms of the quasiparticles they create.
L000272	
L000273	In particular, a Kraus operator acting on a topological state generally creates a superposition of different clusters of anyons, i.e. $\mathcal{K}_{k,i}=\sum_\alpha c_{k,\alpha}\, \mathcal{A}_i[\alpha]$, where $c_{k,\alpha}$ is a complex coefficient, and $\mathcal{A}_i[\alpha]$ creates a cluster of anyons in the vicinity of site $i$~\footnote{Strictly speaking, such a decomposition is well-defined for fixed-point states where the sizes of anyons are zero. In more general situations, e.g., Laughlin states, one can regard this decomposition as a \emph{definition} of phenomenological error models that we choose to work with.}.
L000274	
L000275	Here, we focus on Kraus operators that are dominated by a single term rather than a coherent superposition, i.e. $\mathcal{K}_{k,i}$ is approximately proportional to a certain $\mathcal{A}_i[\alpha]$.
L000276	
L000277	Such Kraus operators are said to create incoherent errors~\footnote{For example, one type of incoherent error creates a single anyon pair, an anyon and its anti-particle, on neighboring sites.}.
L000278	
L000279	The resulting mixed state is an incoherent mixture of states with different anyon configurations.
L000280	
L000281	An essential step in characterizing the phases of the corrupted state is to regard the density matrix as a state vector in a double Hilbert space, i.e.
L000282	\begin{align}
L000283		\kket{\rho} = \mathcal{N} \ket{\Psi_0} \otimes \ket{\Psi_0^*}
L000284	 = \prod_i \sum_{k} \calK_{k,i} \bar{\calK}_{k,i}\ket{\Psi_0} \otimes \ket{\Psi_0^*},
L000285	 \label{eq:EFD definition}
L000286	\end{align}
L000287	where we define barred operators as acting on the bra space, e.g. a super-operator $\calK (\cdot)\calK^\dagger$ acting on a density matrix is denoted as $\calK\,\bar{\calK}$ when acting on $\kket{\rho}$.
L000288	
L000289	We refer to $\kket{\rho}$ as an \emph{errorfield double} (EFD) because of its analogy to the thermofield double state~\cite{Haag:1992hx}.
L000290	
L000291	In the absence of decoherence, $\kket{\rho}$ contains two decoupled copies of conjugate topologically ordered states.
L000292	
L000293	The non-unitary evolution affected by the operator $\mathcal{N}$ can induce phase transitions at finite error rates that can be diagnosed by operator expectation values in the EFD.
L000294	
L000295	Up to normalization, these expectation values are of the form $\bbra{\rho}O\,\bar{O} \kket{\rho} = \tr(\rho O\rho O^\dagger)$.
L000296	
L000297	This object is the overlap between $\rho$ and $\rho'= O\rho O^\dagger$, which measures the distinguishability between these two states.
L000298	
L000299	\emph{Decoherence-induced transition as anyon condensation.}---
L000300	To begin, we consider the example of the Toric code~\cite{Kitaev:1997wr} subject to bit-flip errors.
L000301	
L000302	The Kraus operator $X_i=m_r m_{r'}$ is incoherent and creates two $m$ anyons at neighboring locations $r,r'$.
L000303	
L000304	In the EFD, the corresponding super-operator creates two neighboring anyon pairs, $X_i\bar{X}_i=(m\bar{m})_r (m\bar{m})_{r'}$, where each pair $m\bar{m}$ creates an anyon of the double Toric code.
L000305	
L000306	The resulting EFD is given by
L000307	\begin{equation}
L000308	\kket{\rho} = \exp\left(\mu \sum_{\langle r r'\rangle}(m\bar{m})_r (m\bar{m})_{r'}\right) \ket{\Psi_0}\otimes \ket{\Psi_0^*},
L000309	\end{equation}
L000310	where $\mu = \tanh^{-1}[p/(1-p)]$.
L000311	
L000312	The operation of the error channel generates an effective imaginary time evolution of the pure topological state.
L000313	
L000314	As shown in the supplementary material~\cite{SOM}, this leads to anyon condensation of $m\overline{m}$ at $p_c^{(2)} = 0.178$, with the solvable transition in the 2D (classical) Ising universality class~\footnote{The superscript of $p_c^{(2)}$ indicates the transition in EFD is detected by the quadratic function of the density matrix. For the general $n$-th moment of the density matrix, the critical point $p_c^{(n)}$ depends on $n$. It approaches the error-correction threshold $p_c = 0.109$ in the replica limit $n \to 1$~\cite{Diagnosticstoappear}.}~\footnote{The existence of a transition in the decohered mixed state at a finite error rate is in stark contrast with the destruction of 2D topological order in the Gibbs state at any finite temperature. In the double state $\kket{\rho}$, a far-separated pair of anyons is created with an exponentially decaying probability, while such an anyon pair occurs with a finite probability in the Gibbs ensemble regardless of the distance. The suppressed probability for separated anyons prevents logical errors and is the reason for the robustness of topological order against local decoherence.}.
L000315	
L000316	The anyon condensation can be detected by the open string operator
L000317	\begin{equation}
L000318	    \langle\!\langle W_{m\overline{m}}(\calP)\rangle\!\rangle := \frac{\bbra{\rho} W_{m\overline{m}}(\calP) \kket{\rho}}{\bbrakket{\rho}{\rho}} = \frac{\tr\rho\, w_m(\calP)\, \rho\, w_m^\dagger(\calP)}{\tr \rho^2},
L000319	    \label{eq:OS}
L000320	\end{equation}
L000321	where $w_{m}(\calP) := \prod_{i \in \calP} X_i$, and $W_{m\bar{m}} = w_m \otimes w_m^*$ creates the paired anyon $m\overline{m}$ at each end of the open path $\calP$~\footnote{We remark that the condensate can also be generally probed by the Fredenhagen-Marcu (FM) order parameter of $m\bar{m}$~\cite{Fredenhagen:1983sn,Fredenhagen:1985ft,Fredenhagen:1987gi,Gregor:2010ym,Verresen:2020dmk}.}.
L000322	
L000323	In the topological phase, this quantity decays exponentially with the distance between two endpoints.
L000324	
L000325	In other words, the density matrix obtained by injecting an extra pair of far-separated anyons into $\rho$ is orthogonal to $\rho$ and hence distinguishable from it.
L000326	
L000327	In this sense, the $m$ anyons remain well-defined excitations.
L000328	
L000329	Once the paired anyon condenses, the open string saturates to a non-vanishing value at a long distance.
L000330	
L000331	Other types of incoherent errors in the Toric code, e.g. Pauli-Z or multi-qubit Pauli errors, can induce distinct phases with condensed electric anyon pairs $e\bar{e}$ or fermion pairs $f\bar{f}$.
L000332	
L000333	All of these transitions are mutually independent because the paired anyons are mutual bosons, and they occur at the same $p_c^{(2)}$.
L000334	
L000335	If two types of paired anyons condense simultaneously, it implies a condensate of the third type as well because each two are equivalent (i.e. fuse) to the third type, e.g. $e\times m=f$.
L000336	
L000337	We remark that the anyon condensation can be also probed by the topological entanglement entropy (TEE) of the EFD~\cite{Kitaev:2005dm,LevinWen2005}, which changes discontinuously at the transition.
L000338	
L000339	In the Toric code without decoherence, the TEE is $2\log 2$, with each of the two states contributing $\log 2$.
L000340	
L000341	The condensation of $m\bar{m}$ reduces the TEE to $\log 2$, and further condensing also $e\bar{e}$ completely removes the TEE~\cite{SOM}.
L000342	
L000343	The results in the above example can be readily extended to general Abelian topological orders subject to incoherent errors.
L000344	
L000345	The paired anyon $\alpha\bar{\alpha}$ in the EFD are self-bosons and may condense at a large error rate.
L000346	
L000347	Moreover, different types of paired anyons, $\alpha\bar{\alpha}$ and $\beta\bar{\beta}$, are always mutual bosons and can condense independently.
L000348	
L000349	Anyon condensation in the EFD affects the information encoding in the corrupted state.
L000350	
L000351	In topological quantum memory, the information is manipulated by the logical operator $w_\alpha(\ell)$ that moves an anyon $\alpha$ along non-contractible loops $\ell = \ell_1,\ell_2$ on the torus.
L000352	
L000353	These logical operators are non-commuting and therefore encode quantum information.
L000354	
L000355	In the condensed phase of $\alpha\bar{\alpha}$, $W_{\alpha\bar{\alpha}}$ acting on the EFD does not produce an orthogonal state, i.e. the two states have a finite overlap, indicating the loss of information encoded in $w_\alpha$.
L000356	
L000357	Furthermore, the condensation identifies $W_{\beta\bar{\beta}}$ with $W_{\eta\bar{\eta}}$ provided that $\alpha \times \beta = \eta$.
L000358	
L000359	Thus, two logical operators $w_\beta(\ell)$ and $w_\eta(\ell)$ are no longer independent.
L000360	
L000361	By analyzing the commutation relations between independent logical operators, one can determine the encoding ability.
L000362	
L000363	If the remaining operators are non-commuting, the corrupted state remains a quantum memory, otherwise, only classical information can be encoded.
L000364	
L000365	The memory is completely destroyed if no operators remain.
L000366	
L000367	\emph{Effective field theory.---} To generalize the decoherence-induced transitions to other topologically ordered states and gain insight into the nature of the ensuing phases, we formulate the problem within an effective field theory.
L000368	
L000369	To this end, we employ the path integral representation of the normalization
L000370	\begin{equation}
L000371	\bbrakket{\rho}{\rho} = \bbra{\rho_0} \, \calN^\dagger \calN \, \kket{\rho_0}\, ,
L000372	\end{equation}
L000373	which plays the role of a generating function for expectation values in the EFD.
L000374	
L000375	Since $\kket{\rho_0} = \ket{\Psi_0} \otimes \ket{\Psi_0^*}$ is a double topologically ordered state, it is given as a double TQFT in the Euclidean half spacetime $\tau<0$.
L000376	
L000377	Similarly, $\bbra{\rho_0}$ is represented by a double TQFT in $\tau>0$.
L000378	
L000379	Therefore, in the absence of decoherence, the norm $\bbrakket{\rho_0}{\rho_0}$ is represented by two decoupled TQFT in the entire spacetime.
L000380	
L000381	The decoherence, represented by $\calN^\dagger \calN$, creates a temporal defect in the double TQFT, which couples the conjugate copies on the plane $\tau=0$ [\figref{fig:efd}(b)].
L000382	
L000383	We note that the effect of measurements on a quantum ground state is similarly represented by a temporal defect in the Euclidean action~\cite{Garratt:2022ycp}.
L000384	
L000385	The transition induced by the coupling at $\tau = 0$ must be a boundary transition.
L000386	
L000387	This renders a distinction between the condensation of $\alpha\bar{\alpha}$ in the EFD and that in a quantum ground state.
L000388	
L000389	The latter involves the proliferation of $\alpha\bar{\alpha}$ loops of arbitrary size in the $(2+1)$D spacetime, while the former is marked by the proliferation of small loops of the condensed object $\alpha\overline{\alpha}$, fluctuating near $\tau = 0$ [Fig.~\ref{fig:efd}(b)].
L000390	
L000391	The distinction has consequences on the signatures of the decoherence-induced anyon condensate.
L000392	
L000393	In the Toric code example, the usual ground-state condensation of $m\bar{m}$ would lead to confinement of anyon $e$ that has non-trivial mutual statistics with $m\bar{m}$~\cite{Burnell2017Review}.
L000394	
L000395	This would be detected, for example, by the Wilson loop $W_e(\calC) := \prod_{i\in \calC} Z_i \otimes \mathbb{1}$ along a contractible loop $\calC$.
L000396	
L000397	Once $m\bar{m}$ is condensed, $W_e(\calC)$ is affected by a finite density of large $m\bar{m}$ loops that pierce $\calC$, leading to exponential decay of $W_e(\calC)$ with an area law, i.e. $W_e(\calC)\sim e^{-b \text{Area}(\calC)}$.
L000398	
L000399	By contrast, the decoherence-induced condensate only involves the proliferation of small $m\bar{m}$ loops that only affect $W_e(\calC)$ along its perimeter leading to perimeter-law scaling, $W_e(\calC) \sim e^{-a|\calC|}$, regardless of the error rate.
L000400	
L000401	Thus, the anyon condensation in the EFD does not lead to confinement, and the Wilson loop cannot probe the transition.
L000402	
L000403	\emph{Mapping to (1+1)D boundary phases.---}
L000404	To classify the possible decoherence-induced phases it is convenient to perform a $\pi/2$-spacetime rotation of the (2+1)D TQFT, $\tau \to -x$ and $x \to \tau$.
L000405	
L000406	The bulk action is invariant under this rotation~\cite{Witten:1988TQFT,Fendley_2007, Shankar_2011}, which maps a problem with a temporal defect to a ground state with a spatial defect [\figref{fig:efd}(c)].
L000407	
L000408	The incoherent errors translate to hermitian perturbations in the parent Hamiltonian of this ground state ~\cite{SOM}, localized on the defect line [Fig.~\ref{fig:efd}(d)].
L000409	
L000410	The decoherence-induced phases are therefore mapped to the quantum phases of a one-dimensional defect in a double topologically ordered state, which can be equivalently formulated as boundary phases of a quadrupled state~\cite{BeigiShor2011,Kapustin2011,SOM}.
