L000452	
L000453	The possible phases can be understood using the field theory description.
L000454	
L000455	The Lagrangian characterizing the low-energy physics has three contributions
L000456	\begin{equation}
L000457	\calL = \calL_0 + \calL_{1} + \calL_{\calN}.
L000458	\end{equation}
L000459	$\calL_0$ describes the low-energy excitations in the TQFT, which involve only the edge degrees of freedom since the bulk is gapped.
L000460	
L000461	$\calL_1$ represents the coupling between the edge modes on the left and right of the defect in Fig.~\ref{fig:efd}(d), which stems from the continuity of the TQFTs in the two half spacetimes before the rotation.
L000462	
L000463	This is the coupling ensuring that the system before decoherence is described by a gapped theory without temporal boundaries.
L000464	
L000465	Lastly, $\calL_\calN$ represents the effect of the error channels, which couple the edge modes in the ket and bra copies.
L000466	
L000467	We now focus on the case of Abelian topological order, which is captured by Abelian Chern-Simons theories.
L000468	
L000469	The low-energy excitations are described by a theory of compact bosons on the edge~\cite{Elitzur:1989nr,Wen:1995qn}
L000470	\begin{align}
L000471	\mathcal{L}_0[\phi] = \frac{1}{4\pi} \sum_{I,J} \mathbb{K}_{IJ}^{(2)} \ri\partial_\tau \phi^I \partial_y \phi^J - \mathbb{V}_{IJ}^{(2)} \partial_y \phi^I \partial_y \phi^J,
L000472	\label{eq:edge_lagrangian}
L000473	\end{align}
L000474	where $\phi := [\bar{\phi}_R,\phi_R,\phi_L,\bar{\phi}_L]$, $\phi_{s}$ and $\bar{\phi}_{s}$ are the field variables in the ket and bra Hilbert spaces, respectively, while $s = L,\,R$ denote the two copies of the EFD.
L000475	
L000476	A central object of the theory is the $K$-matrix $\mathbb{K}^{(2)} = K \oplus (-K) \oplus K \oplus (-K)$ with $K$ an integer-valued matrix.
L000477	
L000478	$\mathbb{V}^{(2)} = V \oplus V \oplus V \oplus V$ with $V$ a non-universal positive definite matrix.
L000479	
L000480	In this theory, $\calL_1$ is a non-linear coupling of $\phi_{L}$ to $\phi_R$ and $\bar{\phi}_L$ to $\bar{\phi}_R$, and $\mathcal{L}_\calN$ couples field variables within the same copy of the EFD, i.e. $\phi_s$ and $\bar{\phi}_s$.
L000481	
L000482	The gapped phase of the defect is obtained by condensing bosonic excitations.
L000483	
L000484	The excitations in $\calL_0$ are of the form $e^{\ri\bm{l}^T\cdot \phi}$ labeled by an integer vector $\bm{l}$.
L000485	
L000486	Such excitations are bosonic if their self statistics $\theta_{\bm{l}} := \pi \bm{l}^T (\mathbb{K}^{(2)})^{-1}\bm{l} = 0 \mod 2\pi$.
L000487	
L000488	For Abelian topological order, the condensed bosonic excitations on the edge form a group termed the Lagrangian subgroup~\cite{Levin:2013gaa,Barkeshli:2013,Wang:2012am}.
L000489	
L000490	The possible edge phases are classified by inequivalent Lagrangian subgroups $\calM$.
L000491	
L000492	In our case, the allowed subgroup is subject to symmetry constraints, i.e. it is invariant under the global symmetry $\calG^{(2)} = \mathbb{Z}_2 \times \mathbb{Z}_2^\mathbb{H}$ of the defect theory.
L000493	
L000494	Here, $\mathbb{Z}_2$ is an anti-unitary symmetry associated with the Hermitian conjugation in the double Hilbert space, i.e. $\phi_R^I(\bar{\phi}_R^I) \leftrightarrow \phi_L^I(\bar{\phi}_L^I)$ and $\ri \leftrightarrow -\ri$, and $\mathbb{Z}_2^{\mathbb{H}}$ is an anti-unitary symmetry originating from the hermiticity of density matrix and acts as $\phi_{s}^I \leftrightarrow \bar{\phi}_{s}^I$ with $s = L, R$ and $\ri \leftrightarrow -\ri$~\footnote{The K-matrix $\mathbb{K}^{(2)}$ flips sign under the anti-unitary symmetry, however, the Lagrangian $\calL_0$ is invariant as the minus sign cancels that from the imaginary identity.}~\footnote{The effective theory for the norm of the EFD $\bbrakket{\rho}{\rho} = \tr\rho^2$ is expected to have a $\mathbb{Z}_2$ unitary symmetry permuting two copies of density matrix. This permutation is a combination of the Hermitian conjugation of the EFD and that of the density matrix and thus is included in $\calG^{(2)}$.}.
L000495	
L000496	Furthermore, the interaction only creates specific excitations: paired anyon $\alpha_s \overline{\alpha}_s = e^{\ri \bm{l}^T\cdot (\phi_s-\bar{\phi}_s)}$ by the incoherent errors, and $\alpha_L\alpha'_R = e^{\ri \bm{l}^T\cdot (\phi_L + \phi_R)}$, $\bar{\alpha}_L\bar{\alpha}'_R = e^{-\ri \bm{l}^T\cdot (\bar{\phi}_L +\bar{\phi}_R)}$ by $\calL_1$.
L000497	
L000498	
L000499	
L000500	Hence, the edge phases are governed by the $\calM$ in which the condensed objects are the fusion of such paired anyons.
L000501	
L000502	The Lagrangian subgroup $\calM$ must satisfy the following criteria:
L000503	\begin{enumerate}
L000504		\item $e^{\ri \theta_{\bm{m}\bm{m'}}} = 1, \forall \bm{m}, \bm{m'} \in \calM$;
L000505		\item $\forall \bm{l} \notin \calM$, $\exists \bm{m}$ s.t. $e^{\ri \theta_{\bm{ml}}} \neq 1$;
L000506	        \item $\forall \bm{m} \in \calM$, $ g\bm{m} g^{-1} \in \calM, \forall g \in \calG^{(2)}$;
L000507		\item (Incoherent error) $[\bm{1},\bm{1},-\bm{1},-\bm{1}]^T \cdot \bm{m} = 0 \mod \mathbb{K}^{(2)}\Lambda, \forall \bm{m} \in \calM$~\footnote{The equation is held modulo physical excitations, where $\Lambda$ is an integer vector. $\bm{1}$ is a vector with each component being $1$ and of the same dimension as the matrix $K$.}.
L000508	\end{enumerate}
L000509	Here, $\theta_{\bm{m}\bm{l}} := 2\pi \bm{m}^T (\mathbb{K}^{(2)})^{-1} \bm{l}$ characterizes the mutual statistics between two anyons.
L000510	
L000511	In the three examples, the Toric code, double semion model~\cite{LevinGu:2012}, and $\nu = 1/3$ Laughlin state~\cite{Laughlin1983}, we enumerate the possible phases (i.e. Lagrangian subgroups) summarized in Table~\ref{tab:toric_code_and_double_semion_phases}~\cite{SOM}.
L000512	
L000513	\emph{Discussion.---}
L000514	We have introduced the errorfield double formulation to characterize the breakdown of quantum memories described by Abelian topological orders, generalizing error recovery transitions in specific quantum codes.
L000515	
L000516	We showed that the breakdown of quantum memories corresponds to a boundary transition in a replicated theory consisting of $n\geq 2$ copies of the topological order and its conjugate.
L000517	
L000518	We focused on the $n=2$ case and incoherent errors and classified the possible phases.
L000519	
L000520	Our results are ripe for multiple lines of generalization.
L000521	
L000522	One useful extension is to characterize the effect of quantum channels that include coherent errors~\cite{Bravyi2018Coherent,Flammia2019QECCoherent,Preskill2020Coherent,Venn:2022kxy,Behrends:2022feh}.
L000523	
L000524	A physically important example is provided by amplitude damping errors applied to the Toric code~\cite{Poulin2017,Poulin2018}.
L000525	
L000526	Here, the decoherence involves terms that can drive condensation of anyons in a single-copy Hilbert space (i.e. $\alpha$ or $\bar{\alpha}$).
L000527	
L000528	The corresponding Lagrangian subgroup does not need to satisfy the fourth criterion, allowing for the establishment of additional decoherence-induced phases~\cite{SOM}.
L000529	
L000530	Another future direction is to investigate the effect of decoherence in non-Abelian topologically ordered states.
L000531	
L000532	Here, decoherence can affect the capacity to perform protected quantum computation in the fusion space~\cite{Nayak:2008zza}.
L000533	
L000534	One complication is that errors are intrinsically ``coherent" as incoherent errors can generate anyons in the individual ket or bra copy due to non-Abelian fusion rules.
L000535	
L000536	The existence of an error recovery threshold suggests that the decoherence-induced phases can be characterized by information-theoretic properties.
L000537	
L000538	In a separate work, we propose information-theoretic diagnostics of the topological order in corrupted mixed states and demonstrate their consistency in a concrete example of the Toric code under incoherent errors~\cite{Diagnosticstoappear}.
L000539	
L000540	The EFD framework allows computing quadratic functions of the density matrix and therefore corresponds to the  second R\'enyi versions of the information-theoretic quantities.
L000541	
L000542	However, the framework is generalizable to a replicated theory of the (1+1)D defect, allowing us to analyze the $n$-th moment of $\rho$ (see supplementary material~\cite{SOM}).
L000543	
L000544	The information-theoretic quantities are obtained in the limit $n\to 1$.
L000545	
L000546	We expect that the classification in Table~\ref{tab:toric_code_and_double_semion_phases} still holds in this limit~\cite{SOM}.
L000547	
L000548	Finally, the EFD formalism can be applied to the investigation of mixed states beyond topologically ordered systems.
L000549	
L000550	Decoherence-induced transitions may occur in other states that encode information nonlocally, such as fracton systems~\cite{Haah:2011drr,Vijay:2016phm} and quantum low-density parity check codes~\cite{Breuckmann:2021yvk}.
L000551	
L000552	The recently proposed average symmetry-protected topological phases~\cite{Ma:2022pvq,lee2022symmetry,zhang2022strange} is another problem that can be possibly characterized in the EFD formulation.
