L000867	\section{Phase transition in Toric code subject to incoherent errors}
L000868	In this section, we provide details of the phase transition in the Toric code subject to bit-flip errors.
L000869	
L000870	In Sec.~\ref{sec:transition}, we show that the anyon condensation transition in the EFD is exactly solvable.
L000871	
L000872	It occurs at $p_c^{(2)} = 0.178$ and is of the 2D classical Ising universality.
L000873	
L000874	
L000875	
L000876	
L000877	
L000878	
L000879	In Sec.~\ref{sec:TEE}, we compute the topological entanglement entropy in the EFD state and show it sharply changes from $2\log 2$ to $\log 2$ at the condensation transition.
L000880	
L000881	\subsection{Critical point}\label{sec:transition}
L000882	As mentioned in the main text, the bit-flip errors in the Toric code can induce a condensation of $m\bar{m}$-anyon in the EFD at a finite error threshold.
L000883	
L000884	The transition is exactly solvable and of 2D classical Ising universality.
L000885	
L000886	Here, we provide details on exactly solving the transition.
L000887	
L000888	To start, we consider the EFD state describing the Toric code subject to bit-flip (Pauli-X) errors
L000889	\begin{equation}
L000890	\label{eq:EFD_TC_bitflip}
L000891	    \kket{\rho} = \prod_{l} \left( (1-p) + p X_l\otimes X_l \right) \ket{\Psi_0}\otimes \ket{\Psi_0^*}\, \propto e^{\mu \sum_i X_l \otimes X_l} \ket{\Psi_0}\otimes \ket{\Psi_0^*},
L000892	\end{equation}
L000893	where $p$ is the bit-flip error rate, $\mu = \tanh^{-1}[p/(1-p)]$, $l$ labels the edges in the square lattice, and $\ket{\Psi_0}$ is one of the Toric code ground states specified below.
L000894	
L000895	We work in a loop picture of the Toric code ground state
L000896	\begin{align}
L000897	\ket{\Psi_0} \propto \prod_p \frac{1 + B_p}{2} \ket{+} \propto \sum_{\{g\}} \ket{g},
L000898	\end{align}
L000899	where $p$ in the subscript labels the plaquette, $\ket{+} := \prod_i \ket{+}_i$ is a product state with each qubit initialized in the $+1$ eigenstate of Pauli-X operator, i.e. $X_i \ket{+} = \ket{+}$.
L000900	
L000901	A product of $B_p$ gives $g_z$, which is a product of the Pauli-Z operators along a closed loop.
L000902	
L000903	When acting on $\ket{+}$, $g_z$ flips qubits into $-1$ eigenstates of $X_i$ along a loop $g$ and leads to a product state $\ket{g} := g_z \ket{+}$.
L000904	
L000905	This allows writing the ground state in terms of closed loops $g$, represented by binary variables.
L000906	
L000907	The EFD can be written in the loop picture accordingly.
L000908	
L000909	Before applying the error channel, the double ground state is expressed as
L000910	\begin{align}
L000911	\kket{\rho_0} = \ket{\Psi_0} \otimes \ket{\Psi_0^*} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{\calZ(0)}}\sum_{g,\bar{g}} \kket{g, \bar{g}},
L000912	\end{align}
L000913	where $\kket{g, \bar{g}} := (g_z \otimes \bar{g}_z) \ket{+}\otimes \ket{+}$, $g$ and $\bar{g}$ label two independent loop configurations in the ket and bra copy, respectively.
L000914	
L000915	The error channel assigns a weight in the summation of loops and leads to
L000916	\begin{align}
L000917	    \kket{\rho} = \frac{1}{\sqrt {\calZ(\mu)}}\sum_{g,\bar{g}} e^{-2\mu|g+\bar{g}|}\kket{g,\bar g},
L000918	\end{align}
L000919	
L000920	where $g+\bar{g}$ is a binary sum and represents the relative loop configuration, $\calZ(\mu)$ is the normalization factor, and $\mu$ can be interpreted as the loop tension.
L000921	
L000922	We note that only the relative loop $h := g+\bar{g}$ acquires a loop tension.
L000923	
L000924	Hence, it is more convenient to rewrite the EFD as
L000925	\begin{align}
L000926	    \kket{\rho} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{\calZ(\mu)}} \sum_{g,h} e^{-2\mu|h|}\kket{g,h}.
L000927	\end{align}
L000928	
L000929	The anyon condensation transition is detected by the open string operator
L000930	\begin{align}
L000931	\langle\!\langle W_{m\overline{m}}(\calP)\rangle\!\rangle = \frac{\bbra{\rho} w_{m}(\calP) \otimes w_{\overline{m}}(\calP) \kket{\rho}}{\bbrakket{\rho}{\rho}} = \frac{1}{\calZ(\mu)}\sum_{g,h} (-1)^{\# \text{crossing}(\calP,h)} e^{-4\mu|h|},
L000932	\end{align}
L000933	where $\# \text{crossing}(\calP,h)$ counts the number of crossings between the open string $\calP$ and loop $h$.
L000934	
L000935	In this expression, the open string $\langle\!\langle W_{m\overline{m}}(\calP)\rangle\!\rangle$ can be interpreted as an observable in a statistical mechanical model of loops.
L000936	
L000937	The normalization factor $\calZ(\mu)$ is the partition function $\calZ(\mu) = \Omega(0)\Omega(\mu)$, where the partition function $\Omega(0)$ of $g$ loops is a constant independent of $\mu$, and the partition function of $h$ loop, $\Omega(\mu) = \sum_h e^{-H(\mu)}$, has an effective Hamiltonian $H(\mu) = 4\mu|h|$.
L000938	
L000939	We can identify the loop configuration $h$ with domain walls in a spin model with Ising degrees of freedom living on the plaquettes, i.e.
L000940	\begin{align}
L000941	\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.75, baseline={(current bounding box.center)}]
L000942	\definecolor{myred}{RGB}{240,83,90};
L000943	\definecolor{myblue}{RGB}{93,123,189};
L000944	\small
L000945	\foreach \y in {0,0.7,1.4,2.1}{
L000946		\draw[black!35] (-0.2,\y) -- (2.2,\y);
L000947	}
L000948	\foreach \x in {0,0.7,1.4,2.1}{
L000949		\draw[black!35] (\x,-0.2) -- (\x,2.2);
L000950	}
L000951	\draw[myblue,line width=0.6] (-0.2,0.7) -- (0.7,0.7) --  (0.7,1.4) -- (2.1,1.4) -- (2.1,2.2);
L000952	\draw[myblue,line width=1.5] (0.7,1.4) -- (1.4,1.4);
L000953	\filldraw (1.05,1.05) circle (0.03) node[right]{\scriptsize$\sigma_i$};
L000954	\filldraw (1.05,1.8) circle (0.03) node[right]{\scriptsize$\sigma_j$};
L000955	\draw[dotted,line width=1] (1.05,1.05) -- (1.05,1.8);
L000956	\node[myblue] at (0.3,1.8) {$h_{l}$};
L000957	\end{tikzpicture}, \qquad h_l = \frac{1 - \sigma_i\sigma_j}{2}.
L000958	\end{align}
L000959	
L000960	Then, the loop model reduces to the square lattice Ising model up to a constant
L000961	\begin{align}
L000962	H = \sum_{\langle i, j\rangle} -2\mu \sigma_i\sigma_j.
L000963	\end{align}
L000964	Thus, the condensation transition in the EFD is of the 2D classical Ising universality class.
L000965	
L000966	The critical point is exactly solvable and determined by the Kramers-Wannier duality, $\mu_c = (1/4)\log(1+\sqrt{2})$,
L000967	i.e. $p_c^{(2)} = 0.178$.
L000968	
L000969	
L000970	We remark that this critical point is associated with the partition function $\calZ(\mu) = \tr \rho^2$ and indicates the singularity in the second moment of $\rho$.
