L001010	\subsection{Topological entanglement entropy}\label{sec:TEE}
L001011	Another diagnostic of the anyon condensation is the topological entanglement entropy (TEE) of the EFD state.
L001012	
L001013	In the case of the Toric code subject to incoherent errors, the EFD state can be written as the ground state of a gapped local Hamiltonian.
L001014	
L001015	One can therefore use TEE to detect the transition~\cite{Kitaev:2005dm,LevinWen2005}.
L001016	
L001017	In this subsection, we first construct the parent Hamiltonian of the EFD.
L001018	
L001019	We then compute the R\'enyi-$n$ entropy of the EFD and show that the subleading topological term undergoes a transition from $2\log 2$ to $\log 2$ upon the condensation of $m\bar{m}$ regardless of the R\'enyi order $n$.
L001020	
L001021	This indicates a singularity also in the topological term of von Neumann entropy ($n\to 1$).
L001022	
L001023	We remark that, in a related problem of the Toric code undergoing imaginary time evolution, the TEE changes sharply from $\log 2$ to zero across the phase transition as shown in Ref.~\cite{CastelnovoPRB2008}.
L001024	
L001025	
L001026	The EFD describing the Toric code subject to bit-flip errors is the ground state of a local parent Hamiltonian~\footnote{We thank Meng Cheng for pointing this out to us.}
L001027	\begin{align}
L001028	    \tilde{H} = \sum_{s} V^{-1} h_s V V h_s V^{-1} + \sum_p V^{-1} h_p V V h_p V^{-1},
L001029	\end{align}
L001030	where $V = e^{\mu \sum_i X_i \otimes X_i}$.
L001031	
L001032	The Hamiltonian term $h_s$ runs over $(1 - A_s\otimes \bbI)/2$ and $(1 - \bbI \otimes A_s)/2$, and $h_p$ runs over  $(1 - B_p\otimes \bbI)/2$ and $(1 - \bbI \otimes B_p)/2$.
L001033	
L001034	Both $h_s$ and $h_p$ are projectors.
L001035	
L001036	In the case without decoherence, the EFD for the Toric code pure state is the zero-energy ground state of the Hamiltonian
L001037	\begin{align}
L001038	    H_0 = \sum_s h_s + \sum_p h_p = \sum_s \frac{1 - A_s\otimes \bbI}{2} + \frac{1 - \bbI \otimes A_s}{2} + \sum_p \frac{1 - B_p\otimes \bbI}{2} + \frac{1 - \bbI \otimes B_p}{2}.
L001039	\end{align}
L001040	
L001041	
L001042	Here, the eigenvalue of $\tilde{H}$ is non-negative. One can show $\kket{\rho}$ is a zero-energy eigenstate of $\tilde{H}$ and therefore is the ground state.
L001043	
L001044	It is then legitimate to use the TEE to characterize the topological order in the EFD.
L001045	
L001046	The TEE sharply changes when the gap of $\tilde{H}$ closes.
L001047	
L001048	We now compute the TEE in the EFD and show it undergoes a transition when tuning the error rate.
L001049	
L001050	First, we show the TEE takes distinct values in two limits, the error rate $p = 0$ and $p = 1/2$.
L001051	
L001052	To gain an understanding away from these two limits, we then consider the R\'enyi-$n$ entropy and map it to the free energy cost of spin pinning in a classical spin model.
L001053	
L001054	We show its subleading term undergoes a transition at the critical point and take the analytic limit $n \to 1$ to extract the behavior of the TEE.
L001055	
L001056	
L001057	
L001058	
L001059	We remark that, as far as the entanglement entropy of a finite disk is concerned, the boundary condition of the total system is not important and will not be specified explicitly throughout the entire calculation.
L001060	
L001061	In the limits $p = 0, 1/2$, the EFD state is a stabilizer state, and the TEE can be easily determined.
L001062	
L001063	In the absence of errors, the EFD contains two copies of decoupled Toric code states and exhibits a TEE $\gamma = 2 \log 2$.
L001064	
L001065	In the case of $p = 1/2$, the tension $\mu \to \infty$ imposes the stabilizers $X_i\otimes X_i$ in the EFD.
L001066	
L001067	The resulting EFD is the mutual eigenstate of stabilizers
L001068	\begin{equation*}
L001069	\{A_s \otimes \mathbb{I}, \quad \mathbb{I} \otimes A_s, \quad X_i \otimes X_i, \quad B_p \otimes B_p\}.
L001070	\end{equation*}
L001071	For a simply connected region $A$, its entanglement entropy is given by
L001072	\begin{align}
L001073	    S_A/\log 2 = 2|A| - \# \text{stabilizer}(\text{supp}(s) \in A)
L001074	\end{align}
L001075	where $2|A|$ counts the total number of degrees of freedom in region $A$, and $\# \text{stabilizer}(\text{supp}(s) \in A)$ is the number of stabilizers whose support is completely in $A$.
L001076	
L001077	One can show $S_A = (|\partial A|-1)\log 2$, and the TEE $\gamma = \log 2$.
L001078	
L001079	The distinct values of TEE in two limits indicate its potential singularity as a function of error rate.
L001080	
L001081	To understand the TEE away from two limits, we consider the R\'enyi-$n$ entropies of a simply connected region $A$ and develop a classical spin model description.
L001082	
L001083	The first step is to write down the reduced density matrix.
L001084	
L001085	Here, we separate the loop configuration $g$ ($h$) into two parts $g_A$ and $g_{\bar{A}}$ ($h_A$ and $h_{\bar{A}}$), which support on region $A$ and its complement $\bar{A}$, respectively.
L001086	
L001087	To form a closed loop configuration, two parts must agree on the boundary of $A$, i.e., $\partial g_A = \partial g_{\bar A} = b$, and $\partial h_A = \partial h_{\bar A} = c$.
L001088	\begin{equation*}
