L001121	The reduced density matrix can be written as
L001122	\begin{align}
L001123	    \varrho_A(\mu) &= \Tr_{\bar{A}} \kket{\rho(\mu)}\bbra{\rho(\mu)}  \nonumber \\
L001124	
L001125	    &= \frac{1}{\calZ(\mu)} \sum_{b, c} \sum_{\substack{g_A,\tilde{g}_A,\\\partial g_A = \partial \tilde{g}_A = b}} \sum_{\substack{
L001126	    h_A, \tilde{h}_A, \\\partial h_A = \partial \tilde{h}_A = c}} \Omega_{\bar{A}}(0; b)\Omega_{\bar{A}}(\mu; c)  e^{-2\mu|h_A| - 2\mu|\tilde{h}_A|} \kket{g_A, h_A}\bbra{\tilde{g}_A, \tilde{h}_A},
L001127	\end{align}
L001128	where $\Omega_{\bar{A}}(0;b)$ and $\Omega_{\bar{A}}(\mu;c)$ are the partition function of $g$ and $h$ loops in region $\bar{A}$ with a given boundary condition $b$ and $c$, respectively,
L001129	\begin{align}
L001130	    \Omega_{\bar{A}}(\mu;c) = \sum_{h_{\bar{A}}, \, \partial h_{\bar{A}} = c} e^{-4\mu|h_{\bar{A}}|}.
L001131	\end{align}
L001132	
L001133	The normalization factor $\calZ(\mu) = \Omega(0) \Omega(\mu)$ can be written accordingly as
L001134	\begin{align}
L001135	     \Omega(\mu) = \sum_c \Omega_{A}(\mu;c) \Omega_{\bar{A}}(\mu;c).
L001136	
L001137	\end{align}
L001138	
L001139	Next, we write down the $n$-th moment of the reduced density matrix
L001140	\begin{align}
L001141	\Tr \varrho_A^n(\mu) = \Gamma_{A}(0) \Gamma_{A}(\mu),
L001142	\end{align}
L001143	where $\Gamma_{A}(0)$ and $\Gamma_A(\mu)$ are associated with $g$ and $h$ loops, respectively, and
L001144	\begin{align}
L001145	    \Gamma_{A}(\mu) = \frac{1}{\Omega^n(\mu)}\sum_{c}\Omega_{A}^n(\mu;c) \Omega_{\bar{A}}^n(\mu;c).\label{eq:n_moment}
L001146	\end{align}
L001147	
L001148	Equation~\eqref{eq:n_moment} can be viewed as a ratio between two partition functions.
L001149	
L001150	The denominator represents the partition function of $n$ copies of loop models, while the loop model in the numerator is further subject to the constraint that loops in different copies have the same boundary condition $c$.
L001151	
L001152	The R\'enyi entropy therefore can also be interpreted as the excess free energy related to the above constraint
L001153	\begin{align}
L001154	S_A^{(n)} = \frac{1}{1-n} \log \Tr\varrho_A^n(\mu) = \frac{1}{n-1}\left( F_A^{(n)}(0) - nF_0(0)\right) + \frac{1}{n-1}\left( F_A^{(n)}(\mu) - nF_0(\mu)\right)
L001155	\end{align}
L001156	where $F_0(\mu) = -\log\Omega(\mu)$ is the free energy of the loop model with tension $\mu$, $F_A^{(n)}(\mu) = -\log \sum_{c}\Omega_A^n(\mu;c)\Omega_{\bar{A}}^n(\mu;c)$ is the free energy of $n$ copies of the loop model subject to the constraint.
L001157	
L001158	The scaling of the excess free energy is easier to analyze when formulating the loop model in terms of Ising spins.
L001159	
L001160	We again identify the loop configuration with Ising domain walls
L001161	
L001162	\begin{align}
L001163	g^{(s)}_l = \frac{1 - \sigma_i^{(s)}\sigma_j^{(s)}}{2}, \quad h_l^{(s)} = \frac{1 - \sigma'^{(s)}_i\sigma'^{(s)}_j}{2},\quad s = 1,2,\cdots, n
L001164	\end{align}
L001165	
L001166	Furthermore, we redefine the Ising variables
L001167	\begin{align}
L001168	    \sigma_i^{(s)} := \sigma_i^{(1)}\tau_i^{(s-1)}, \quad \sigma'^{(s)}_i := \sigma'^{(1)}_i\tau'^{(s-1)}_i,\quad s = 2, 3,\cdots, n.
L001169	\end{align}
L001170	
L001171	The constraints on the loop configurations require $n$ copies of the Ising spins, $\sigma^{(s)}$ and $\sigma'^{(s)}$, on the boundary of the region $A$ having the same domain wall configurations.
L001172	
L001173	Thus, they require the relative Ising spin $\tau^{(s)}_i$ and $\tau'^{(s)}_i$ having no domain wall on the boundary of $A$, i.e. pointing in the same direction.
L001174	
L001175	There are $(n-1)$ such constraints in $F_A^{(n)}(\mu)$, therefore $F_A^{(n)}(\mu) = nF_0(\mu) + (n-1)\Delta F(\mu)$, where $\Delta F$ is the excess free energy for aligning one copy of the $\tau$ spins on the boundary of $A$.
L001176	
L001177	Correspondingly, the R\'enyi entropy $S_A^{(n)} = \Delta F(0) + \Delta F(\mu)$.
L001178	
L001179	The excess free energy has two contributions.
L001180	
L001181	The energetic part is always proportional to $|\partial A|$.
L001182	
L001183	The entropic part counts the loss of degrees of freedom due to spin aligning.
L001184	
L001185	In the ferromagnetic phase, the spins can fluctuate below the scale of correlation length $\xi$.
L001186	
L001187	Thus, $\Delta F \propto |\partial A|/\xi$ exhibits a strict area-law scaling.
L001188	
L001189	In the paramagnetic phase, the spins can both fluctuate freely above the scale of correlation length $\xi$.
L001190	
L001191	At the leading order, the free energy cost is proportional to $|\partial A|$.
L001192	
L001193	However, the aligned spin can fluctuate globally giving rise to a subleading correction, i.e. $\Delta F = a|\partial A| - \log 2$.
L001194	
L001195	Thus, the subleading term sharply changes at the ferromagnetic transition.
L001196	
L001197	The above analysis indicates the R\'enyi entropy exhibits an area-law scaling $S_A^{(n)} = a |\partial A| - \gamma^{(n)}$, and the topological term $\gamma^{(n)}$ distinguishes two phases of the effective spin model.
L001198	
L001199	When $\mu < \mu_c$, both spin models for $\sigma$ and $\sigma'$ are in the paramagnetic phase, giving rise to $\gamma^{(n)} = 2\log 2$.
L001200	
L001201	When $\mu > \mu_c$, the spin model for $\sigma'$ (i.e. the loop model for $h$) is in the ferrmagnetic phase, reducing $\gamma^{(n)}$ to $\log 2$.
L001202	
L001203	The result holds for an arbitrary R\'enyi order $n$ and also in the replica limit $n \to 1$.
L001204	
L001205	Hence, the TEE of the EFD state sharply changes from $2\log 2$ to $\log 2$ when $m\bar{m}$ condenses at $p_c^{(2)}$.
L001206	
L001207	We make an important remark that the effective model for the R\'enyi-$n$ entropy of the EFD is merely $n$ decoupled loop models.
L001208	
L001209	The partition function $\calZ^n(\mu) = (\tr\rho^2)^n$.
L001210	
L001211	Therefore, the transition occurs at the same critical threshold $p_c^{(2)}$ regardless of $n$.
L001212	
L001213	This is fundamentally different from the transition in the $n$-th moment $\tr \rho^n$ of the corrupted density matrix, which occurs at a different critical threshold $p_c^{(n)}$.
L001214	
L001215	The Toric code state subject to Pauli-Y and Z errors can be similarly analyzed.
L001216	
L001217	These errors can induce a condensation of $f\bar{f}$ and $e\bar{e}$ in the EFD at the same critical threshold $p_c^{(2)}$.
L001218	
L001219	We also note that, paired anyons $\alpha\bar{\alpha}$ and $\beta\bar{\beta}$ are mutual bosons and therefore can condense independently.
L001220	
L001221	When two types of anyons are condensed, e.g. $e\bar{e}$ and $m\bar{m}$, the rest one $f\bar{f}$ is also condensed as $e \times m = f$.
L001222	
L001223	The resulting EFD no longer contains condensed loop objects and will exhibit a vanishing TEE.
