L001378	
L001379	We consider the EFD for the Abelian topologically ordered ground state subject to incoherent errors.
L001380	
L001381	The ground state $\ket{\Psi_0}$ is described by a $U(1)^M$ Chern-Simons theory with the following Lagrangian
L001382	\begin{equation}
L001383	    \calL_{CS}[a] = \frac{1}{4\pi} \sum_{I,J = 1}^{M} K_{IJ}\, a^I \wedge \rd a^J + \cdots \,,
L001384	\end{equation}
L001385	where $K$ is a symmetric $M \times M$ integer matrix and $a^{I=1,2,\ldots,M}$ are the $U(1)$ gauge fields, the ellipsis denotes parts that involve matter fields~\cite{Read:1990zz,Wen:1992uk,Frohlich:1991wb,Wen:1995qn}.
L001386	
L001387	Matter fields are gapped and do not enter the dynamics at long distances, yet, they are necessary in giving rise to the anyonic excitations.
L001388	
L001389	Accordingly, $\ket{\Psi_0^*}$ carries the conjugate topological order and is described by the same Lagrangian except that the $K$-matrix has an additional minus sign. To better distinguish them, we use $\bar{a}^{I=1,2,\ldots,M}$ to denote the gauge fields for $\ket{\Psi_0^*}$.
L001390	
L001391	We can combine these two parts and write the path integral $\calZ_{\tau<0}$ or $\calZ_{\tau>0}$ in terms of the following Lagrangian
L001392	\begin{equation}
L001393	    \calL_{CS}[\mathbf{a}] = \frac{1}{4\pi} \sum_{I,J = 1}^{2M} \mathbb{K}_{IJ}\, \mathbf{a}^I \wedge \rd \mathbf{a}^J + \cdots,
L001394	\end{equation}
L001395	where $\mathbb{K} = K \oplus (-K)$, and $\mathbf{a} = [a, \bar{a}]^T$ is a $2M$ component vector of the gauge fields in the Hilbert space $\calH\otimes \calH$.
L001396	
L001397	
L001398	
L001399	
L001400	
L001401	Studying the decoherence-induced phases and the potential phase transitions in the EFD in the current form is not convenient.
L001402	
L001403	On one hand, in general, anyonic excitations created by local errors explicitly involve matter fields and are complicated to analyze.
L001404	
L001405	On the other hand, the transition, which happens at the temporal interface, is different from a ground state problem and largely unexplored.
L001406	
L001407	These technical difficulties necessitate our following trick.
L001408	
L001409	We perform a spacetime rotation to exchange the imaginary time $\tau$ and the spatial coordinate $x$ [Fig.~1(c) of the main text], i.e.,
L001410	\begin{equation}
L001411	    \tau \rightarrow  -x\,,\quad
L001412	    x \rightarrow \tau\,,\quad
L001413	    y\rightarrow y\,,
L001414	\end{equation}
L001415	where $x,y$ denote the spatial coordinates.
L001416	
L001417	The rotation turns the temporal interface at $\tau = 0$ into a spatial one at $x = 0$.
L001418	
L001419	The original path integral \eqref{eq:path_int} can be formally rewritten as
L001420	\begin{equation*}
L001421	\bbrakket{\rho}{\rho} = \int \calD(\varphi_L, \bar{\varphi}_L, \varphi_R, \bar{\varphi}_R) \, \calZ_{x>0}[\varphi_R, \bar\varphi_R] \calZ_{x<0}[\varphi_L, \bar\varphi_L] e^{- \tilde \calS_{int}(\varphi_R,\bar\varphi_R,\varphi_L,\bar\varphi_L)}\,,
L001422	\end{equation*}
L001423	where $\calZ_{x<0}$, obtained by rotating $\calZ_{\tau<0}$, is the path integral on the left side of the spatial interface $x = 0^-$, and $(\varphi_L,\bar{\varphi}_L)$ specify the value of the fields at the spatial interface. The meaning of $\calZ_{x>0}$ and $(\varphi_R,\bar{\varphi}_R)$ is similar.
L001424	$\tilde \calS_{int}$ describes the coupling between the two half systems at the spatial interface and generally takes a different form from the original one $\calS_{int}$.
L001425	
L001426	After the rotation, the path integral is translationally invariant in the new time direction and describes the norm of a $(2+1)$D quantum ground state [Fig.~1(c) of the main text].
L001427	
L001428	In this rotated picture, the decoherence channel introduces a 1D defect at $x = 0$ that couples the two sides, and the decoherence-induced phases correspond to the $(1+1)$D phases of the 1D defect.
L001429	
L001430	However, it is not obvious that the evolution in the new time direction is generated by a Hermitian Hamiltonian.
L001431	
L001432	
L001433	
L001434	In what follows, we will show that for the case of incoherent errors considered in this work, the path integral indeed describes the ground state of a Hermitian Hamiltonian.
L001435	
L001436	The incoherent channel takes the following form when acting on the EFD,
L001437	\begin{align}
L001438	    \calN = \prod_i \calN_i, \quad \calN_i = \sum_k \calK_{k,i}\bar{\calK}_{k,i} = \sum_k |c_{k}|^2\calA_i[\alpha_k]\bar{\calA}_i[\alpha_k],
L001439	\end{align}
L001440	where the Kraus operator $\calK_{k,i} = c_k \calA_i[\alpha_k]$ for incoherent error creates a cluster of anyons labeled by $\alpha_k$ in the vicinity of site $i$.
L001441	
L001442	For example, it can create anyon $\alpha_k$ and its anti-particle on neighboring sites.
L001443	
L001444	A key observation is that $\calK_{k,i}\bar{\calK_{k,i}}$ only creates or hops paired anyons $\alpha_k\bar{\alpha}_k$ in the EFD with a positive real weight $|c_k|^2$.
L001445	
L001446	
L001447	
L001448	
L001449	
L001450	
L001451	
L001452	
L001453	
L001454	
L001455	
L001456	
L001457	
L001458	
L001459	
L001460	
L001461	
L001462	
