L001468	
L001469	The Lagrangian that describes the 1D defect has three contributions
L001470	\begin{align}
L001471	    \calL = \calL_0 + \calL_1 + \calL_{\calN},
L001472	\end{align}
L001473	where $\calL_0$ describes the low energy edge excitations for the $(2+1)D$ theory in the region $x > 0$ and $x < 0$.
L001474	
L001475	The interaction on the defect is derived from $\tilde{S}_{int}$ and contains two parts.
L001476	
L001477	The first part originates from the definition of path integral and couples degrees of freedom on the left and the right given by $\calL_1$, which is present even in the absence of a quantum channel.
L001478	
L001479	The second part $\calL_\calN$ is introduced by the quantum channel.
L001480	
L001481	In what follows, we explicitly write down the three terms in the Lagrangian and analyze the possible phases.
L001482	
L001483	We start with the low energy edge theory $\calL_0$ for the CS theory in the bulk.
L001484	
L001485	We consider how the path integral for the past $\calZ_{\tau<0}$ changes under the rotation:
L001486	\begin{itemize}
L001487	\item Originally, $\calZ_{\tau<0}[\varphi_p, \bar{\varphi}_p]$ describes the path integral that prepares the ground state of a system on a whole plane, where $\varphi_p, \bar{\varphi}_p$ specifies the component of the wavefunction. Note that $\varphi_p$ and $\bar{\varphi}_p$ includes both the CS gauge fields and the matter fields, and thus they are complicated to deal with.
L001488	
L001489	\item The spacetime rotation transforms it into $\calZ_{x>0}[\varphi_R, \bar{\varphi}_R]$, a path integral defined on the half-space but the entire time domain, and $\varphi_R, \bar{\varphi}_R$ specifies the boundary value of the fields.
L001490	
L001491	The bulk part of the path integral contains the same CS action as that before the rotation and the part involving matter fields.
L001492	
L001493	The boundary has a simple description in terms of compact bosons, which is the main reason why the rotation trick can be useful.
L001494	
L001495	\item From now on, we will replace $\varphi_R$ and $\bar{\varphi}_R$ by the compact boson fields $\phi_R, \bar{\phi}_R$, which correspond to the bulk gauge fields $a, \bar{a}$.
L001496	Then, the low-energy dynamics of the boundary of the CS theory is governed by the following effective Lagrangian
L001497	\begin{equation}
L001498	    \calL_R[\Phi_R] = \frac{1}{4\pi} \sum_{I,J=1}^{2M} -\mathbb{K}_{IJ} \ri \partial_\tau \Phi_R^I \partial_y \Phi_R^J - \mathbb{V}_{IJ} \partial_y \Phi_R^I \partial_y \Phi_R^J,
L001499	\end{equation}
L001500	where $\Phi_R := [\phi_R, \bar{\phi}_R]$, $\mathbb{K}$ is the same K-matrix as the one for the bulk CS action, and $\mathbb{V}$ is a positive semi-definition matrix that determines the velocity of the boson fields.
L001501	The path integral with fixed boundary values of the boson fields is then determined by $\calL_R[\Phi_R]$ classically:
L001502	\begin{equation}
L001503	    \calZ_{x<0}[\Phi_R] = \exp\left( - \int \rd\tau \rd y \calL_R[\Phi_R] \right)\,.
L001504	\end{equation}
L001505	\end{itemize}
L001506	The transformation of the path integral for the future $\calZ_{\tau>0}$ can be analyzed similarly. The result is related to what we have obtained above by a spatial reflection
L001507	\begin{equation*}
L001508	    \calL_L[\Phi_L] = \frac{1}{4\pi} \sum_{I,J=1}^{2M} \mathbb{K}_{IJ} \ri \partial_\tau \Phi_L^I \partial_y \Phi_L^J - \mathbb{V}_{IJ} \partial_y \Phi_L^I \partial_y \Phi_L^J,
L001509	\end{equation*}
L001510	where $\Phi_L := [\phi_L, \bar{\phi}_L]$, and the minus sign in front of the $K$-matrix comes from the spatial reflection.
L001511	The path integral $\calZ_{x>0}[\Phi_L]$ can also be determined classically
L001512	\begin{equation}
L001513	    \calZ_{x>0}[\Phi_L] = \exp\left( - \int \rd\tau \rd y \calL_L[\Phi_L] \right)\,.
L001514	\end{equation}
L001515	
L001516	
L001517	
L001518	
L001519	
L001520	
L001521	We combine the low-energy excitations from the left and the right and obtain the Lagrangian describing the edge of the quadruple topological order
L001522	\begin{equation}
L001523	    \mathcal{L}_0[\phi] = \frac{1}{4\pi} \sum_{I,J} \mathbb{K}_{IJ}^{(2)} \ri \partial_\tau \phi^I \partial_y \phi^J - \mathbb{V}_{IJ}^{(2)} \partial_y \phi^I \partial_y \phi^J\,,\label{eq:edge_lagrangian}
L001524	\end{equation}
L001525	where the $4M\times 4M$ $K$-matrix $\mathbb{K}^{(2)} = \mathbb{K} \oplus \mathbb{K}$, velocity matrix $\mathbb{V}^{(2)} = \bbV\oplus\bbV$, and $\phi := [\bar{\phi}_R, \phi_R, \phi_L, \bar{\phi}_L]$.
L001526	
L001527	
L001528	
L001529	
L001530	
L001531	
L001532	
L001533	
L001534	Next, we discuss the interaction in the effective theory.
L001535	
L001536	The first part $\calL_1$ is associated with the definition of path integral and couples $\phi_L$ to $\phi_R$ and $\bar{\phi}_L$ to $\bar{\phi}_R$.
L001537	
L001538	It leads to a gapped phase of the defect in the absence of errors.
L001539	
L001540	The second part $\calL_\calN$ is introduced by the error channel.
L001541	
L001542	It couples the bosonic fields from the same copy of the density matrix (or equivalently EFD), namely $\phi_s$ to $\bar{\phi}_s$ for $s = L, R$.
L001543	
L001544	To explicitly write down the interaction in terms of the compact bosons, we first list the excitations in the effective theory $\calL_0$.
L001545	
L001546	The excitations are labeled by integer vectors $\bm{l}_\alpha$ and are created by $e^{\ri\bm{l}_\alpha^T\cdot \phi}$.
L001547	
L001548	They have anyonic statistics, and the braiding of two anyons $\bm{l}_\alpha$ and $\bm{l}_\beta$ yields a phase
L001549	\begin{equation}
L001550	\theta_{\alpha\beta} = 2\pi \bm{l}_\alpha^T (\mathbb{K}^{(2)})^{-1} \bm{l}_\beta.
L001551	\end{equation}
L001552	
L001553	We remark that, in general, a single anyon cannot be created using a local operator.
L001554	
L001555	Instead, a pair of anyon $\alpha$ and its antiparticle $\alpha'$ can be created using a local operator, which is described by $\Psi_{\Lambda} := e^{\ri (\mathbb{K}^{(2)} \Lambda)^T \cdot \phi}$ with $\Lambda$ being an integer vector.
L001556	
L001557	Such excitation has trivial braiding with other excitations, i.e. $\theta_{\alpha, \mathbb{K}^{(2)}\Lambda} = 0 \mod 2\pi, \forall \alpha$.
L001558	
L001559	
L001560	
L001561	
L001562	
L001563	
L001564	Furthermore, the form of the interaction is constrained by the global $\calG^{(2)} = \mathbb{Z}_2 \times \mathbb{Z}_2^\mathbb{H}$ symmetry of the 1D defect.
L001565	
L001566	The first $\mathbb{Z}_2$ is an anti-unitary symmetry generated by the hermitian conjugation in the double Hilbert space.
L001567	
L001568	The path integral represents the norm of the EFD, which is a real number.
L001569	
L001570	Thus, the effective theory is invariant under the transformation
L001571	
L001572	\begin{equation}
L001573	\begin{aligned}
L001574	    \phi_{L}^I &\leftrightarrow \phi_{R}^I, \quad \bar{\phi}_{L}^I \leftrightarrow \bar{\phi}_{R}^I.
L001575	\end{aligned}
L001576	\end{equation}
L001577	
L001578	In addition, the EFD represents the density matrix, which is hermitian in the original Hilbert space.
L001579	
L001580	Thus, edge theory exhibits another anti-unitary $\mathbb{Z}_2^\mathbb{H}$ symmetry due to the hermiticity of the density matrix, which acts as
L001581	\begin{equation}
L001582	\begin{aligned}
L001583	    \phi_{s}^I &\leftrightarrow \bar{\phi}_{s}^I,\quad s = L,R.
L001584	\end{aligned}
L001585	\end{equation}
L001586	We remark that the K-matrix $\mathbb{K}^{(2)}$ flips the sign under the anti-unitary symmetry, however, the Lagrangian $\calL_0$ is invariant as the minus sign cancels that from the imaginary identity.
L001587	
L001588	
L001589	We are now ready to explicitly write down the interaction in terms of edge excitations.
L001590	
L001591	The first part $\calL_1$ from the definition of path integral can be written in terms of the back-scattering between the left and the right fields.
L001592	
L001593	It is generally given by a hopping term between the left and the right
L001594	\begin{align}
L001595	    \sum_{\Lambda} \frac{B_{\Lambda}(y)e^{\ri \theta(y)}}{2} \left(\Psi_{L,\Lambda}^\dagger \Psi_{R,\Lambda} + \bar{\Psi}_{R,\Lambda}^\dagger \bar{\Psi}_{L,\Lambda}\right) + h.c.,
L001596	\end{align}
L001597	where $\Psi_{L,\Lambda} := e^{\ri (K\Lambda)^T \phi_L}$, $\Psi_{R,\Lambda} := e^{-\ri (K\Lambda)^T \phi_R}$, $\bar{\Psi}_{L,\Lambda} := e^{\ri (-K\Lambda)^T \bar{\phi}_L}$, and $\bar{\Psi}_{R,\Lambda} := e^{\ri (K\Lambda)^T \bar{\phi}_R}$ are local operators on the edge, $\Lambda$ is a $M$-component integer vector, the real coefficient $B_\Lambda$ and phase factor $\theta$ have spatial dependence in general.
L001598	
L001599	
L001600	
L001601	
L001602	
L001603	In terms of the compact boson fields, the back-scattering takes the form
L001604	\begin{equation}
L001605	    \mathcal{L}_1 = \sum_\Lambda B_\Lambda \Big[ \cos\Big(\sum_{I = 1}^M (K\Lambda)_I (\phi_L^I + \phi_R^I) +\theta \Big) + \cos\Big(\sum_{I = 1}^M (K\Lambda)_{I}(\bar{\phi}_L^I + \bar{\phi}_R^I)+\theta\Big)\Big].\label{eq:edge_int_ref}
L001606	\end{equation}
L001607	
L001608	We remark that the coefficients for two cosine terms are forced to the same real number due to the global symmetry $\mathcal{G}^{(2)}$.
L001609	
L001610	With large coefficients, such cosine terms tend to pin the bosonic fields $\phi+\bar{\phi}$ and lead to a gapped phase of the defect without any error channel.
L001611	
L001612	These interaction terms only create anyon bound states on the left and the right in the form $\alpha_L\alpha'_R = e^{\ri\bm{l}^T\cdot(\phi_L + \phi_R)}$ and $\bar{\alpha}_L\bar{\alpha}'_R = e^{-\ri\bm{l}^T\cdot(\bar{\phi}_L + \bar{\phi}_R)}$.
L001613	
L001614	The second part $\calL_\calN$ of the interaction is introduced by the quantum channel and couples the fields from the same copy of the density matrix.
L001615	
L001616	
L001617	
L001618	
L001619	
L001620	For incoherent error channels, before the rotation, $\calN^\dagger \calN$ only creates paired anyons $\alpha\bar{\alpha}$ in the EFD.
L001621	
L001622	Since $\calN^\dagger \calN$ is a Hermitian operator in the double space, an anyon cluster of $\alpha\bar{\alpha}$ and its Hermitian conjugate that involves its anti-particle $\alpha'\bar{\alpha}'$ are created with the same (positive real) weight.
L001623	
L001624	Therefore, in the rotated picture, the quantum channel introduces local couplings $e^{\ri(K\Lambda)^T(\phi_s - \bar{\phi}_s)}$ and $e^{-\ri(K\Lambda)^T(\phi_s - \bar{\phi}_s)}$ ($s = L, R$) with the same real weights, which combine to the cosine terms at the 1D defect,
L001625	
L001626	
L001627	
L001628	
L001629	
L001630	\begin{align}
L001631	    \mathcal{L}_{\mathcal{N}} = \sum_\Lambda C_\Lambda \sum_{s = L, R} \cos\left( \sum_{I = 1}^M (K\Lambda)_I (\phi_{s}^I - \bar{\phi}_{s}^I) \right),\label{eq:int_quantum_channel}
L001632	\end{align}
L001633	where $C_\Lambda$ is a real coefficient with spatial dependence.
L001634	
L001635	
L001636	
L001637	The coefficients of the cosine terms for $s = L, R$ are the same due to the $\mathbb{Z}_2$ symmetry.
L001638	
L001639	
L001640	
L001641	We note that the cosine terms in the Lagrangian all have real coefficients.
L001642	
L001643	After the canonical quantization in the rotated picture, the edge Hamiltonian is Hermitian.
L001644	
L001645	This guarantees that the decoherence-induced phases indeed map to ground-state phases on the edge.
L001646	
L001647	
L001648	
L001649	
L001650	
L001651	
L001652	
L001653	The possible phases of the defect are classified by the inequivalent ways of gapping out $\calL_0$ in Eq.~\eqref{eq:edge_lagrangian} by condensing bosonic excitations.
L001654	
L001655	For Abelian topological order, the bosonic objects form a group, dubbed the Lagrangian subgroup $\calM$~\cite{Levin:2013gaa,Barkeshli:2013,Wang:2012am}.
L001656	
L001657	Thus, the phases of Abelian topological order subject to incoherent errors are classfied by the subgroup $\calM$ that satisfying the following criteria:
L001658	\begin{enumerate}
L001659		\item $e^{\ri \theta_{\bm{m}\bm{m'}}} = 1, \forall \bm{m}, \bm{m'} \in \calM$;
L001660		\item $\forall \bm{l} \notin \calM$, $\exists \bm{m}$ s.t. $e^{\ri \theta_{\bm{ml}}} \neq 1$;
L001661	        \item $\forall \bm{m} \in \calM$, $ g\bm{m} g^{-1} \in \calM, \forall g \in \calG^{(2)}$;
L001662		\item (Incoherent error) $[\bm{1}, \bm{1},-\bm{1},-\bm{1}]^T \cdot \bm{m} = 0 \mod \mathbb{K}^{(2)}\Lambda, \forall \bm{m} \in \calM$,
L001663	\end{enumerate}
L001664	where $\Lambda$ is a $4M$-component integer vector, $\bm{1}$ is an $M$-component vector with each element being unity.
L001665	
L001666	Here, the third criterion originates from the symmetry constraints, i.e. the excitations related by symmetry transformations must condense simultaneously.
L001667	
L001668	The last criterion is due to incoherent errors.
L001669	
L001670	The interaction on the edge only creates anyon bound states $\alpha_L\bar{\alpha}_L$, $\alpha_R\bar{\alpha}_R$, $\alpha_L\alpha'_R$, $\bar{\alpha}_L\bar{\alpha}'_R$ and their fusion results.
L001671	
L001672	Therefore, the condensed objects satisfy the last criterion.
L001673	
L001674	In the case of coherent errors, the phases are still classified by the Lagrangian subgroup.
L001675	
L001676	However, since the Kraus operators can, for example, create anyon excitations $\alpha_s$ and $\bar{\alpha}_s$ in a single copy, the condensed objects need not satisfy the last criterion.
L001677	
L001678	
L001679	
L001680	Correspondingly, in the Lagrangian, the coherent errors can introduce the cosine terms, $\cos((K\Lambda)^T \cdot \phi_{s})$ and $\cos((K\Lambda)^T \cdot \bar{\phi}_{s})$, which solely depends on the compact boson in an individual copy of the topological order.
L001681	
L001682	This can possibly lead to additional decoherence-induced phases.
L001683	
L001684	Each edge condensate corresponds to a distinct phase of the EFD, and its condensed objects also determine the capability of encoding information in the error-corrupted state.
L001685	
L001686	In the main text, we mention that the logical operator $W_{\alpha\bar{\alpha}}(\ell)$ can no longer encode quantum information in the condensate of paired anyons $\alpha\bar{\alpha}$ in the EFD, which is equivalent to condensing $\alpha_s\bar{\alpha}_s$ with $s = L,R$ in the $(1+1)$D theory of the defect.
L001687	
L001688	Hence, examining whether $\alpha_s\bar{\alpha}_s$ is condensed on the edge can determine the encoding properties in the error-corrupted state.
L001689	
L001690	\subsection{Examples}\label{sec:examples}
L001691	
L001692	Here, we classify the possible phases by enumerating the Lagrangian subgroups in three examples, 2D Toric code, double semion model, and $\nu = 1/3$ Laughlin state, subject to incoherent errors.
L001693	
L001694	We comment on the information encoding in each phase.
L001695	
L001696	The results are summarized in Table~I of the main text.
L001697	
L001698	\emph{Example 1: Toric code.}---
L001699	The first example we consider is the 2D Toric code subject to incoherent errors.
L001700	
L001701	We find five distinct gapped phases (see Table~I~\footnote{Here and in the following, Table~I refers to the table in the main text.}).
L001702	
L001703	To start with, the low energy excitations in 2D Toric code are described by the CS theory with K-matrix
L001704	\begin{align}
L001705	    K_{TC} = \begin{pmatrix}
L001706	    0 & 2 \\
L001707	    2 & 0
L001708	    \end{pmatrix}.
L001709	\end{align}
L001710	The Toric code has four superselection sectors: $1, e, m, f$. Here, $e$ and $m$ are self-boson and exhibit mutual semionic statistics. The fermion $f$ is a composite object of $e$ and $m$.
L001711	
L001712	These anyonic quasiparticles are labeled by integer vectors
L001713	\begin{align}
L001714	    \bm{l}_e = \begin{pmatrix}
L001715	    1 \\
L001716	    0
L001717	    \end{pmatrix}, \quad \bm{l}_m = \begin{pmatrix}
L001718	    0 \\
L001719	    1
L001720	    \end{pmatrix}, \quad \bm{l}_f = \begin{pmatrix}
L001721	    1 \\
L001722	    1
L001723	    \end{pmatrix}.
L001724	\end{align}
L001725	
L001726	The first gapped phase is achieved when no error is present.
L001727	
L001728	In this case, the definition of path integral introduces the cosine terms in Eq.~\eqref{eq:edge_int_ref}
L001729	\begin{equation}
L001730	\begin{aligned}
L001731	    \calL_{1} = \sum_{I=1,2} B_I \Big[&\cos\left(2(\phi_L^I +  \phi_R^I ) + \theta\right) + (\phi_s \leftrightarrow \bar{\phi}_s) \Big] + \cdots.
L001732	\end{aligned}\label{eq:toric_code_int_ref}
L001733	\end{equation}
L001734	
L001735	
L001736	Here, the ellipses represent the less relevant terms.
L001737	
L001738	With sufficiently large coefficients $B_I$, these cosine terms can induce an edge condensate of $e_{L}e_R, \bar{e}_L\bar{e}_R, m_Lm_R$, and $\bar{m}_L\bar{m}_R$ (Phase I in Table~I).
L001739	
L001740	The corrupted density matrix in this phase can encode quantum information because $\alpha_s\bar{\alpha}_s$ is not condensed.
L001741	
L001742	The incoherent error channel can induce distinct edge phases.
L001743	
L001744	
L001745	Here, we have three cosine terms in the form of Eq.~\eqref{eq:int_quantum_channel}
L001746	\begin{equation}
L001747	\begin{aligned}
L001748	    \mathcal{L}_{\mathcal{N},e} &= C_e\sum_{s = L, R} \cos\left(2(\bm{l}_e^T \cdot \phi_{s}-\bm{l}_e^T \cdot \bar{\phi}_{s})\right),\\
L001749	    \mathcal{L}_{\mathcal{N},m} &= C_m\sum_{s = L, R} \cos\left(2(\bm{l}_m^T \cdot \phi_{s}-\bm{l}_m^T \cdot \bar{\phi}_{s})\right),\\
L001750	    \mathcal{L}_{\mathcal{N},f} &= C_f\sum_{s = L, R} \cos\left(2(\bm{l}_f^T\cdot \phi_{s}-\bm{l}_f^T\cdot \bar{\phi}_{s})\right),
L001751	\end{aligned}\label{eq:toric_code_int_error}
L001752	\end{equation}
L001753	which are generated by the incoherent channels that create $e$, $m$, and $f$, respectively.
L001754	
L001755	With a sufficiently large coefficient, any one of three cosine terms can develop a classical value leading to a condensate of $\alpha_s \bar{\alpha}_s$ with $\alpha = e, m, f$, which corresponds to Phase II-IV in Table~I, respectively.
L001756	
L001757	In these phases, the remaining logical operators mutually commute, and the corrupted state is a classical memory.
L001758	
L001759	Finally, when two cosine terms develop a classical value, the remaining one also achieves a classical value (Phase V in Table~I).
L001760	
L001761	Here, the corrupted state is a trivial state, and one cannot encode any information.
L001762	
L001763	
L001764	
