L001822	For example, $\ket{\Psi_0} \rightarrow e^{\mu \sum_i Z_i}\ket{\Psi_0}$ can realize the edge condensate of $e_s$ and $\bar{e}_s$.
L001823	
L001824	Second, the above two phases are studied in the context of 1D defect phases in double Toric code and are referred to as the ``cut-open" boundary~\cite{Lichtman:2020nuw}.
L001825	
L001826	\emph{Example 2: Double semion.}---
L001827	The second example we consider is the double semion model subject to incoherent errors~\cite{LevinGu:2012}.
L001828	
L001829	Again, we enumerate the possible Lagrangian subgroups that characterize the distinct phases.
L001830	
L001831	We find five phases summarized in Table~I.
L001832	
L001833	The low-energy theory for double semion topological order is the Abelian CS theory with K-matrix
L001834	\begin{align}
L001835	    K_{DS} = \begin{pmatrix}
L001836	    2 & 0 \\
L001837	    0 & -2
L001838	    \end{pmatrix}.
L001839	\end{align}
L001840	The double semion model has four superselection sectors: $1, m_a, m_b, b$, where $m_a$ is a semion, $m_b$ is an anti-semion, and the boson $b$ is a composite object of $m_a$ and $m_b$.
L001841	
L001842	The semion $m_a$ and anti-semion $m_b$ are mutual boson.
L001843	
L001844	These anyons are characterized by the integer vectors
L001845	\begin{align}
L001846	    \bm{l}_{m_a} = \begin{pmatrix}
L001847	    1 \\
L001848	    0
L001849	    \end{pmatrix}, \quad \bm{l}_{m_b} = \begin{pmatrix}
L001850	    0 \\
L001851	    1
L001852	    \end{pmatrix}, \quad \bm{l}_b = \begin{pmatrix}
L001853	    1 \\
L001854	    1
L001855	    \end{pmatrix}.
L001856	\end{align}
L001857	
L001858	The double semion model on the torus has a four-fold degenerate ground state that can encode two logical qubits.
L001859	
L001860	The logical operators are the loop operators $w_\alpha(\ell)$ transporting $\alpha = m_a, m_b$ along two inequivalent large loops $\ell = \ell_1,\ell_2$.
L001861	
L001862	Distinct from the case of Toric code, the operators along different loops, $w_\alpha(\ell_1)$ and $w_\alpha(\ell_2)$ for $\alpha = m_a, m_b$, anti-commute due to the anyon self-statistics and therefore serve as Pauli-X and Z operators for a logical qubit.
L001863	
L001864	The first gapped phase (Phase I in Table~I) of the defect is realized when no error is present.
L001865	
L001866	The backscattering term $\calL_1$ condenses the anyon bound states $\alpha_L{\alpha}'_R$ and $\bar{\alpha}_L\bar{\alpha}'_R$ for $\alpha = m_a, m_b, b$.
L001867	
L001868	This phase can encode two qubits of quantum information.
L001869	
L001870	The incoherent channel in the double semion model can introduce the following cosine terms:
L001871	\begin{equation}
L001872	\label{eq:double_semion_int_error}
L001873	\begin{aligned}
L001874	\mathcal{L}_{\mathcal{N},m_a} &= C_{m_a}\sum_{s = L, R} \cos\left(2(\bm{l}_{m_a}^T\cdot\phi_{s}-\bm{l}_{m_a}^T\cdot\bar{\phi}_{s})\right), \\
L001875	\mathcal{L}_{\mathcal{N},m_b} &= C_{m_b}\sum_{s = L, R} \cos\left(2(\bm{l}_{m_b}^T\cdot\phi_{s}-\bm{l}_{m_a}^T\cdot\bar{\phi}_{s})\right),\\
L001876	\mathcal{L}_{\mathcal{N},b} &= C_b\sum_{s = L, R} \cos\left(2(\bm{l}_{b}^T\cdot\phi_{s}-\bm{l}_{b}^T\cdot\bar{\phi}_{s})\right),
L001877	\end{aligned}
L001878	\end{equation}
L001879	which creates paired anyons $\alpha_s\bar{\alpha}_s$ for $\alpha = m_a, m_b, b$, respectively.
L001880	
L001881	Condensing each one of these terms induces Phase II-IV in Table~I, respectively.
L001882	
L001883	In Phase II [III], one can encode information in $w_{m_b}(\ell)$ [$w_{m_a}(\ell)$] for $\ell = \ell_1,\ell_2$.
L001884	
L001885	These logical operators do not mutually commute and therefore can encode one qubit of quantum information.
L001886	
L001887	
L001888	In Phase IV, the condensation of $b_s\bar{b}_s$ also results in a classical memory~\footnote{We appreciate Zhuan Li and Roger Mong for pointing out a mistake in the early version of our manuscript regarding information encoding in the corrupted double semion states.}.
L001889	
L001890	To understand this, we first recall the logical operators in the double-semion state on the torus, which encodes two-qubit quantum information.
L001891	
L001892	The operators $w_{m_a}(\ell)$ along non-contractible loops $\ell_1$ and $\ell_2$ anti-commute and serve as the logical Pauli-X and Z operator for the first qubit, i.e. $X_1 := w_{m_a}(\ell_1)$ and $Z_1 := w_{m_a}(\ell_2)$.
L001893	
L001894	The operator $w_{m_b}(\ell_1)$ and $w_{m_b}(\ell_2)$ are identified with the logical operators for the second qubit, i.e. $X_2 := w_{m_b}(\ell_1)$ and $Z_2 := w_{m_b}(\ell_2)$.
L001895	
L001896	Condensing $b\bar{b}$ in the doubled state leads to the logical errors $w_b(\ell_1) = X_1X_2$ and $w_b(\ell_2) = Z_1Z_2$ and completely dephase the logical qubits.
L001897	
L001898	The resulting logical density matrix is diagonal in the Bell-state basis, therefore only encodes classical information.
L001899	
L001900	
L001901	
L001902	
L001903	
L001904	
L001905	Condensing two terms in Eq.~\eqref{eq:double_semion_int_error} indicates the third is condensed and results in Phase V, which cannot encode information.
L001906	
L001907	Introducing coherent errors can potentially realize an additional phase by condensing the boson in individual copies, $b_s$ and $\bar{b}_s$.
L001908	
L001909	Again, designing a specific coherent error channel to possibly realize this phase is left for future study.
L001910	
L001911	\emph{Example 3: $\nu = 1/3$ Laughlin state.---}
L001912	The third example is $\nu = 1/3$ Laughlin state~\cite{Laughlin1983} with incoherent errors.
L001913	
L001914	In this case, we find two phases summarized in Table~I.
L001915	
L001916	The low-energy excitations in the Laughlin state is described by the Abelian CS theory with K-matrix $K_{1/3} = (3)$.
L001917	
L001918	It contains three superselection sectors $\{1, \eta, \eta^2\}$.
L001919	
L001920	$\eta$ and $\eta^2$ are the quasiparticle and quasihole, and they are labeled by the charge vector $\bm{l}_\eta = (1)$ and $\bm{l}_{\eta^2} = (2)$ and carry charge $e/3$ and $-e/3$, respectively.
L001921	
L001922	There is only one type of incoherent channel; its Kraus operators create a pair of quasiparticle and quasihole.
L001923	
L001924	When the error is below the threshold, the corrupted state is a quantum memory characterized by the Lagrangian subgroup generated by $\eta_L \eta_R^2$ and $\bar{\eta}_L\bar{\eta}_R^2$ (Phase I in Table~I).
L001925	
L001926	When error proliferates, we obtain a trivial phase with a subgroup generated by $\eta_L \bar{\eta}_L$ and $\eta_R \bar{\eta}_R$ (Phase II in Table~I).
