L001958	
L001959	So far, the analysis focuses on the distinct phases in the EFD, which pertains to analyzing the second moment of the density matrix, i.e. the norm of the EFD $\bbrakket{\rho}{\rho} = \tr \rho^2$.
L001960	
L001961	In this subsection, we generalize the problem to the $n$-th moment $\tr\rho^n$ and map the decoherence-induced phases to $(1+1)$D boundary phases.
L001962	
L001963	Before discussing the $n$-th moment, we develop an equivalent formulation of the second moment $\tr \rho^2$.
L001964	
L001965	The phases of the $(1+1)$D defect can be converted to the $(1+1)$D edge phases by folding around the defect as shown in Fig.~\ref{suppfig:nth_moment}(b)~\cite{BeigiShor2011,Kapustin2011}.
L001966	
L001967	In the folded picture, we have four copies of the topological order.
L001968	
L001969	On the edge, we relabel the bosonic fields as
L001970	\begin{align}
L001971	    \phi := [\bar{\phi}_R, \phi_R, \phi_L, \bar{\phi}_L] = [\phi_1, \bar{\phi}_1, \phi_2, \bar{\phi}_2],
L001972	\end{align}
L001973	where $\phi_{1(2)}$ and $\bar{\phi}_{1(2)}$ denote the fields associated with the copy-$1(2)$ of the density matrix.
L001974	
L001975	The path integral describing the ground state of quadruple topological order with an edge corresponds to an overlap in the quadruple Hilbert space $\calH^{\otimes 4}$ before the rotation
L001976	\begin{align}
L001977	\tr\rho^2 = \bbra{\mathbb{C}^{(2)}}\left(\kket{\rho}\otimes \kket{\rho}\right) := \int \mathcal{D}(\varphi_1, \varphi_2) \left(\bra{\varphi_1}\otimes \bra{\varphi_2}\right)\otimes \kket{\rho} \, \left(\bra{\varphi_2}\otimes \bra{\varphi_1}\right)\otimes \kket{\rho},
L001978	\end{align}
L001979	where $\bbra{\mathbb{C}^{(2)}}$ is a ``swap" reference state that couples the fields in the ket and bra Hilbert space of the different copies of the EFD.
L001980	
L001981	The overlap is readily generalized to describe the $n$-th moment $\tr\rho^n$.
L001982	
L001983	Here, in $2n$ copies of the Hilbert space $\calH^{\otimes 2n}$, we consider
L001984	\begin{align}
L001985	\tr\rho^n = \bbra{\mathbb{C}^{(n)}}\left(\kket{\rho}^{\otimes n}\right) := \int \mathcal{D}(\varphi_1, \varphi_2, \cdots, \varphi_n) \prod_{s = 1}^n \left(\bra{\varphi_s}\otimes \bra{\varphi_{s+1}}\right)\otimes \kket{\rho},
L001986	\end{align}
L001987	where $\varphi_{n+1} := \varphi_1$, and $\bbra{\mathbb{C}^{(n)}}$ is a generalized swap reference state.
L001988	
L001989	We can similarly develop a path integral formulation of the overlap.
L001990	
L001991	In the rotated picture, it corresponds to the ground state of $2n$ copies of the topological order with an $(1+1)$D edge.
L001992	
L001993	The effective edge theory also contains three parts
L001994	\begin{equation}
L001995	\calL^{(n)} = \calL_0^{(n)} + \calL_1^{(n)} + \calL_\calN^{(n)}.
L001996	\end{equation}
L001997	The first part $\calL_0^{(n)}$ describes the low-energy excitations in $2n$ copies of the topological order
L001998	\begin{align}
L001999	\calL_0^{(n)}[\phi] = \frac{1}{4\pi} \sum_{I,J} \mathbb{K}_{IJ}^{(n)} \ri\partial_\tau \phi^I \partial_y \phi^J - \mathbb{V}_{IJ}^{(n)} \partial_y \phi^I \partial_y \phi^J
L002000	\end{align}
L002001	where the compact bosonic field $\phi := [\phi_1, \bar{\phi}_1, \phi_2, \bar{\phi}_2, \cdots, \phi_n, \bar{\phi}_n]$ is a $2nM$-component vector, $\mathbb{K}^{(n)} = [K \oplus (-K)]^{\oplus n}$, and $\mathbb{V}^{(n)} = V^{\oplus 2n}$.
L002002	
L002003	The second part $\calL_1^{(n)}$ is the interaction introduced by the reference state, which takes a general form
L002004	\begin{align}
L002005	\calL_1^{(n)} = \sum_\Lambda B_\Lambda \sum_{s = 1}^n \cos\left( \sum_I (K\Lambda)_I (\phi_s^I + \bar{\phi}_{s+1}^I) + \theta \right).
L002006	\end{align}
L002007	where $\bar{\phi}_{n+1} := \bar{\phi}_1$.
L002008	
L002009	The last part $\calL_\calN^{(n)}$ describes the error channel. The incoherent errors can introduce the following terms similar to Eq.~\eqref{eq:int_quantum_channel}
L002010	\begin{align}
L002011	\calL_\calN^{(n)} = \sum_\Lambda C_\Lambda \sum_{s = 1}^n \cos\left( \sum_I (K\Lambda)_I (\phi_s^I - \bar{\phi}_s^I)\right).
L002012	\end{align}
L002013	
L002014	The edge theory exhibits a global symmetry $\mathcal{G}^{(n)} = \mathbb{Z}_n \times \mathbb{Z}_2^\mathbb{H}$.
L002015	
L002016	We note that the $\mathbb{Z}_n$ symmetry is a unitary symmetry generated by the cyclic permutation over $n$ copies of the density matrix
L002017	\begin{align}
L002018	    \phi_s \leftrightarrow \phi_{s+1}, \quad \bar{\phi}_s \leftrightarrow \bar{\phi}_{s+1}.
L002019	\end{align}
L002020	In the case of $n = 2$, $\calG^{(n)}$ reduces to $\calG^{(2)}$ in Sec.~\ref{sec:mapping} as the permutation over two copies of the density matrix is a combination of the Hermitian conjugation of the EFD and that of the density matrix.
L002021	
L002022	We remark that the incoherent errors can induce condensation of $\alpha_s \bar{\alpha}_s$ for $s = 1, 2, \cdots n$ on the edge.
L002023	
L002024	Since the different paired anyons $\alpha_s \bar{\alpha}_s$ and $\beta_{s'}\bar{\beta}_{s'}$ are mutual bosons, different incoherent channels do not compete with each other.
L002025	
L002026	Thus, the number of possible phases characterized by anyon condensates is independent of the replica index $n$.
L002027	
L002028	
L002029	Hence, we expect that each phase of the EFD (equiv. $\tr\rho^2$) in Table~I has a correspondence in the replica limit $n \to 1$.
